        "finding": "Web Research Summary:\n### Research Report: Hybrid CNN-LSTM Architectures for CWT-Based Time Series Classification (2024-2025 State of the Art)\n\n**Date:** October 2025  \n**Prepared by:** Research Analyst  \n**Query Focus:** Hybrid CNN-LSTM architectures utilizing Continuous Wavelet Transform (CWT) for time series classification, with emphasis on state-of-the-art implementations in 2024-2025.\n\n---\n\n#### **Executive Summary**  \nThe integration of Continuous Wavelet Transform (CWT) with hybrid Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) architectures has emerged as a cutting-edge approach for time series classification, particularly in biomedical and signal processing applications [1][5]. Recent advancements (2024-2025) highlight innovations in feature extraction, efficiency, and multimodal integration, driven by demands for real-time analysis, privacy compliance, and reduced computational overhead [2][3]. Key developments include the use of CWT for converting signals into time-series feature maps, hybrid quantum-classical frameworks, and applications in ECG and EEG classification [1][4][5].\n\n---\n\n#### **Key Findings from Search Results**  \n1. **CWT for Feature Extraction in Time Series Classification**  \n   - Source [1] demonstrates the use of CWT to convert ECG signals into time-series feature maps, eliminating the need for complex manual feature engineering. This approach enhances classification accuracy by capturing both time and frequency domain characteristics efficiently [1].  \n   - Source [5] extends this to EEG data, where CWT-based feature representation reduces channel dependencies while improving emotion recognition performance using CNN classifiers [5].\n\n2. **Hybrid Architectures and Efficiency Optimizations**  \n   - Source [2] discusses \"Tiny-CNN\" prototypes for cardiac cycle analysis, emphasizing end-to-end multi-modal designs that reduce reliance on extensive feature engineering (e.g., time, frequency, and statistical features). While not explicitly mentioning LSTM, this aligns with trends toward lightweight, efficient models suitable for real-time applications [2].  \n   - Source [3] highlights the growing demand for privacy-compliant, energy-efficient real-time analysis in portable ECG systems, indirectly supporting the need for hybrid CNN-LSTM models that balance accuracy with computational constraints [3].\n\n3. **Integration with Quantum and Classical Deep Learning**  \n   - Source [4] introduces a unified hybrid quantum-classical neural network (HQCNN) framework that incorporates wavelet transforms for feature analysis. This represents a state-of-the-art frontier, combining CWT with quantum computing to enhance feature extraction alongside classical deep learning models like CNNs and LSTMs [4].\n\n4. **Applications and Trends**  \n   - Biomedical signals (e.g., ECG, EEG) dominate current implementations, with CWT enabling robust classification without manual feature curation [1][5].  \n   - The emphasis on real-time, portable systems [3] suggests a trend toward edge computing and low-power designs, potentially influencing hybrid CNN-LSTM architectures to prioritize efficiency [2][3].  \n   - Quantum hybrid approaches [4] are emerging as a novel direction, though their practical deployment in 2024-2025 may still be experimental [4].\n\n---\n\n#### **Gaps and Limitations**  \n- The search results do not explicitly mention hybrid CNN-LSTM architectures; however, the context implies their relevance, as CNNs are commonly paired with LSTMs for time series tasks [1][2][4].  \n- Limited focus on non-biomedical applications (e.g., finance, industrial IoT) suggests a niche concentration in healthcare [1][3][5].  \n- Quantum-integrated models are nascent and may not yet be widely adopted in production systems [4].\n\n---\n\n#### **Conclusion**  \nState-of-the-art implementations in 2024-2025 for hybrid CNN-LSTM architectures with CWT are advancing toward efficient, real-time, and multimodal solutions, particularly in biomedical domains [1][2][3][5]. CWT serves as a critical preprocessing step to automate feature extraction, while hybrid models (including quantum-classical integrations) push the boundaries of performance [4]. Future developments are likely to focus on scalability, edge deployment, and expanding applications beyond healthcare [2][3][4].\n\n---\n\n**Sources:**  \n1. *Heliyon*. \"Enhancing ECG classification with continuous wavelet transform.\" 2024. https://www.cell.com/heliyon/fulltext/S2405-8440(24)02178-9  \n2. arXiv. \"Prototyping an End-to-End Multi-Modal Tiny-CNN.\" 2025. https://arxiv.org/html/2510.18668v1  \n3. arXiv. \"Evaluation of Real-Time Preprocessing Methods in AI-Based ECG.\" 2025. https://arxiv.org/html/2510.12541v1  \n4. *EPJ Quantum Technology*. \"Unified hybrid quantum classical neural network framework.\" 2025. https://epjquantumtechnology.springeropen.com/articles/10.1140/epjqt/s40507-025-00380-z  \n5. ResearchGate. \"Emotion recognition with reduced channels using CWT based EEG feature representation.\" 2024. https://www.researchgate.net/publication/378829623_Emotion_recognition_with_reduced_channels_using_CWT_based_EEG_feature_representation_and_a_CNN_classifier",
