        "finding": "Consolidated summary at step 2 (for the specific architecture 'entropy_stabilized_gaf.py'):\n### Strategic Learnings\nThis memory entry consolidates insights from hyperparameter optimization, model performance analysis, and data handling for convolutional neural networks (CNNs) applied to SERS spectral data. Key strategic takeaways emphasize prioritizing architectural depth, balanced tuning, and early convergence monitoring to achieve high accuracy and stability.\n\n#### Hyperparameters\n- **Convolutional Layers and Architecture Depth**: Deeper architectures (e.g., 7 convolutional layers) significantly boost mean accuracy (+0.494) and reduce variability (-0.503 standard deviation), making them ideal for feature extraction in spectral data. **Action**: Favor deeper CNNs for spectral tasks to enhance performance without excessive complexity.\n- **Learning Rate**: Moderate to higher learning rates (e.g., 0.000565) correlate positively with accuracy (+0.504) and learning speed (+0.884), minimizing variability from low rates. **Action**: Increase learning rates from low baselines to expedite convergence, ensuring stability.\n- **Kernel Size and Filters**: Smaller kernels (3) and fewer filters (32) improve generalization by reducing overfitting (-0.538 and -0.257 correlations with accuracy) and enhancing stability. **Action**: Opt for compact kernels and filters to prevent overfitting on tabular spectral features.\n- **Dense Units, Dropout, and Epochs**: Lean dense layers (128 units), moderate dropout (0.185), and fewer epochs (27) yield better accuracy (-0.456 and -0.356 correlations) and faster training. **Action**: Use compact designs and balanced regularization to optimize efficiency and minimize overfitting.\n\n#### Model Performance\n- **Accuracy and Stability**: Top models hit 97.3% mean accuracy with low variance (0.0036 standard deviation), minimal validation loss (0.09), and rapid learning (0.062 speed), far outperforming underperforming mode